<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Washington's crossing of the Delaware River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>'s crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a>, on the night of 25–26 December 1776, was the first move in a surprise attack by the Continental Army on the Hessian forces garrisoned at Trenton during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. Washington, the army's commander-in-chief, led a column from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> across the icy river to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> at McConkey's Ferry, in weather that turned from rain to sleet and snow. Other planned crossings in support were called off or ineffective, leaving about 2,400 men for the attack. After defeating the Hessians under Johann Rall in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trenton">Battle of Trenton</a>, the army crossed back to Pennsylvania with its prisoners and captured stores.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washingtons-crossing-of-the-delaware-river.jpg" alt="George Washington's crossing of the Delaware River" width="330" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Caleb Bingham · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_Crossing_the_Delaware_1856-71_George_Caleb_BinghamFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Through 1776 the Continental Army lost the defence of New York City. William Howe landed on Long Island in August and by mid-November had pushed Washington out of New York entirely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Charles Cornwallis</a> then chased the army across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, after which the British took winter quarters in a chain of outposts from New Brunswick to Burlington, leaving Hessian troops under Colonels Rall and Von Donop at Trenton and Bordentown.</p><p>Washington's army shrank as enlistments expired and men deserted, and most of it crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, destroying or moving every boat for miles in both directions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a>'s pamphlet The American Crisis was published on 19 December, and Washington ordered his troops to read it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 23 December Washington told his staff that he would attack Trenton shortly before sunrise on 26 December, telling Joseph Reed that dire necessity justified the risk. The plan called for three crossings: Washington's own column at McConkey's Ferry, a diversion by John Cadwalader at Dunk's Ferry, and James Ewing's crossing below Trenton to hold the bridge over the Assunpink Creek. Of about 6,000 men fit for duty, many were detailed to guard ferries, supplies and the sick, leaving about 2,400 for the attack. Boats were gathered under Captain Daniel Bray and others, among them Durham boats, and were worked by experienced watermen including John Glover's Marblehead Regiment.</p><p>The army reached the river at about 4 pm on 25 December, but most of the men were not at the crossing point until about 6 pm. Henry Knox directed the crossing, which had to carry horses and eighteen pieces of artillery; the guns were not across until 3 am.</p><p>Ice jams stopped Ewing, and Cadwalader brought back his men when he could not get his artillery over. Washington then marched on Trenton in two columns, one under General John Sullivan. The Hessians were overcome: 22 were killed and 98 wounded against three Americans killed and six wounded, and nearly 1,000 prisoners were taken.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The army crossed back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> with its prisoners, some of its men having drunk captured rum, and then crossed a third time on 29 December and after, at eight points, with the whole army and its baggage back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> by New Year's Eve. Washington beat back an assault at Trenton on 2 January 1777 and won at Princeton the next day. The victory raised the army's morale, and the communities of Washington Crossing in Pennsylvania and in New Jersey are named for the crossing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Washington's crossing of the Delaware River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>The crossing</b> was meant to be one of several; the other crossings planned in support were called off or ineffective, leaving about 2,400 men for the attack.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
